<!-- Curriculum sub-strand: Core 1.4, Essential 1.4 -->

<chapter xml:id="ch-linear-programming" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Linear Programming</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      A farmer in Nakuru has limited land, labour and money, and wants to choose how much maize and how many beans to plant to make the largest profit. A problem like this, where you look for the best choice within given limits, is solved by linear programming. It builds on linear inequalities and their graphs.
    </p>
    <p>
      In this chapter you will form linear inequalities from real-life situations and draw them on a Cartesian plane to show the region of possible choices. You will then find the best, or optimal, solution in that region, and apply the method to real-life problems.
    </p>
    <p>
      Linear programming is used by farmers, manufacturers, transporters and other businesses to keep costs low and profits high when resources are limited.
    </p>
  </introduction>

